---
run: round-trip
fixpoint: executed
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# floor30-atlas-dollar-zero-spellings — QUESTION: what do the script-location suffix/prefix removals
# produce when the value has NO slash at all, when it is `./name`, when it is absolute, and when it
# sits at the root?
#
# Why the inclusion design needs it: `p-x-load-operand-param-expansion-of-dollar-zero` proposes to
# resolve `${0%/*}/dep.sh` inside the engine. The trap is the no-slash case — `%` removes the
# SMALLEST suffix matching the pattern, and a word with no `/` matches nothing, so `${0%/*}` is the
# whole word rather than `.`, and `${0%/*}/dep.sh` becomes `book.sh/dep.sh`. An engine that models
# the expansion as "take the dirname" would resolve a file a real shell never names.
#
# HARNESS LIMIT (`gap-stdin-invoked-book`): the floor rail always executes the manifest as a script
# it names, so the no-slash case cannot be produced through `$0` at all, and `sh < book` — where
# `$0` is the shell's own name — is not expressible by this lane. The pattern semantics below are
# measured on ordinary variables, which is the half that decides the engine's arithmetic.
#
# AND A MEASURED REASON NOT TO ASK ABOUT `$0` ITSELF (`gap-dollar-zero-shape-is-platform-bound`):
# this case once carried a `case $0 in */*)` cell. It was minted under WSL as `[yes]` and then went
# RED on the Windows leg as `[no]` — the rail passes the script path with the platform's own
# separator, so `$0`'s SHAPE is a property of the harness and the OS, not of the dialect. A manifest
# is measure-once ground truth re-checked on every platform, so a platform-bound cell cannot live in
# one. The finding itself is the useful part, and it belongs to
# `p-x-load-operand-param-expansion-of-dollar-zero`: the engine must resolve `${0%/*}` from the
# AUTHORED BOOK PATH it owns, never from whatever spelling a shell would have put in `$0`.
v=book.sh
printf 'noslash: pct-slash-star=[%s]\n' "${v%/*}"
printf 'noslash: pctpct-slash-star=[%s]\n' "${v%%/*}"
printf 'noslash: hashhash-star-slash=[%s]\n' "${v##*/}"
printf 'noslash: joined=[%s]\n' "${v%/*}/dep.sh"
w=./book.sh
printf 'dotslash: pct-slash-star=[%s]\n' "${w%/*}"
printf 'dotslash: joined=[%s]\n' "${w%/*}/dep.sh"
u=/a/b/book.sh
printf 'abs: pct-slash-star=[%s]\n' "${u%/*}"
printf 'abs: joined=[%s]\n' "${u%/*}/dep.sh"
r=/book.sh
printf 'root: pct-slash-star=[%s]\n' "${r%/*}"
printf 'root: joined=[%s]\n' "${r%/*}/dep.sh"

-- expected.emitted --
noslash: pct-slash-star=[book.sh]
noslash: pctpct-slash-star=[book.sh]
noslash: hashhash-star-slash=[book.sh]
noslash: joined=[book.sh/dep.sh]
dotslash: pct-slash-star=[.]
dotslash: joined=[./dep.sh]
abs: pct-slash-star=[/a/b]
abs: joined=[/a/b/dep.sh]
root: pct-slash-star=[]
root: joined=[/dep.sh]

-- expected.ran --

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --results -
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=ca0839ccb323fcc10fce0aef079e1a7178fded4355d5039d1c7376980ac92196 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:3 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:4 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:5 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:6 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:7 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:8 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:9 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:10 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:11 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:12 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:13 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

